      title: 'Initial Presentation',
      content: 'A 55-year-old right-handed male civil engineer presents to the PM&R clinic after EMG/NCS performed by the referring neurologist confirmed findings consistent with ALS. He was referred for rehabilitation evaluation and care coordination. His chief complaints are progressive right hand weakness over 6 months causing difficulty with drafting and typing, fasciculations in both arms and the right thigh, recent onset of tripping on uneven surfaces, and a 10-pound unintentional weight loss. He has not yet been told the diagnosis. The referring neurologist\'s note reads: "EMG shows diffuse denervation with fasciculations in three body regions. Nerve conduction studies show reduced CMAP amplitudes with normal sensory responses. Please discuss diagnosis and initiate multidisciplinary management." His wife and adult son accompany him. He appears anxious and states: "I\'ve been reading online, and I\'m terrified this is ALS."',
