    const Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content: 'Key areas to assess:\n- **Motor symptoms timeline**: Onset with right hand weakness (grip, pinch). Progressive involvement of intrinsic hand muscles, then forearm. Recent tripping suggests early lower extremity involvement. Ask about foot drop, stair difficulty.\n- **Bulbar function**: Speech changes (slurring, nasal quality), dysphagia (choking on liquids vs solids, meal duration changes, drooling), jaw fatigue when chewing. Weight loss may reflect early dysphagia or hypermetabolism.\n- **Respiratory symptoms**: Orthopnea, morning headaches, excessive daytime somnolence, reduced exercise tolerance — all early signs of diaphragmatic weakness. Sleep-disordered breathing. Ask about ability to count to 20 in a single breath.\n- **Fasciculations**: Distribution, timing, whether they preceded weakness.\n- **Negative symptoms (critical for excluding mimics)**: No sensory changes, no bowel/bladder dysfunction, no eye movement abnormalities, no pain (pain argues against pure ALS). No cognitive or behavioral changes (screen for frontotemporal involvement with brief cognitive/behavioral assessment).\n- **Family history**: Any family members with ALS, dementia, or neurodegenerative disease? Approximately 5-10% of ALS is familial.\n- **Functional status**: Handwriting deterioration, difficulty with buttons and zippers, opening jars, utensil use. Work status — still working? Able to perform job duties?\n- **Psychosocial**: Mood screen (PHQ-9). Current support system. Insurance status. Advance directive status. Financial planning concerns.\n- **ALS Functional Rating Scale-Revised (ALSFRS-R)**: Administer the 12-item scale (score 0-48). This is the single most important functional outcome measure in ALS and should be repeated at every visit to track disease progression.',
    ),
    const Section(
      title: 'Physical Examination Findings:',
      content: '**Vitals**: BP 134/78 mmHg, HR 76 bpm, RR 16, SpO2 97% on room air, Weight 172 lbs (down from 182 lbs six months ago), BMI 24.1.\n\n**General**: Well-appearing male, alert, anxious. Normal body habitus with early thenar wasting noted bilaterally (right > left). No respiratory distress at rest.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Cranial Nerves**: II-XII grossly intact. No tongue fasciculations or atrophy. Normal palatal elevation. Normal extraocular movements (sparing of eye movements is characteristic of ALS). Speech clear without dysarthria at this stage. Jaw jerk mildly brisk.\n- **Motor — Upper Extremities**: Right hand intrinsic muscle atrophy (first dorsal interosseous, thenar eminence). Left thenar eminence early flattening. Fasciculations visible in bilateral deltoids and right forearm at rest. Right grip strength 18 kg (reduced), left grip 32 kg. Right finger abduction 3+/5, left 4+/5. Bilateral biceps 5/5, triceps 5/5, deltoids 5-/5. Fine motor: difficulty with rapid finger tapping on the right, impaired pincer grasp.\n- **Motor — Lower Extremities**: Right tibialis anterior 4/5, left 5/5. Right hip flexion 4+/5. Bilateral quadriceps 5/5. Fasciculations visible in right quadriceps and bilateral gastrocnemius.\n- **Tone**: No spasticity in extremities currently. Mildly increased tone at bilateral ankles on rapid dorsiflexion.\n- **Reflexes**: Biceps 3+ bilaterally, triceps 3+ bilaterally, brachioradialis 3+ bilaterally, patellar 3+ bilaterally, Achilles 3+ bilaterally. **Positive Hoffman sign bilaterally. Positive Babinski on the right.** The combination of upper motor neuron signs (hyperreflexia, Hoffman, Babinski) with lower motor neuron signs (atrophy, fasciculations, weakness) is the hallmark of ALS.\n- **Sensory**: Intact to light touch, pinprick, proprioception, and vibration throughout (normal sensory exam is expected in ALS).\n- **Gait**: Mild right foot drop with steppage gait pattern. Tandem gait impaired. No assistive device currently used.\n\n**Respiratory Assessment**: Forced vital capacity (FVC) sitting: 82% predicted. FVC supine: 74% predicted (>10% drop from sitting to supine suggests early diaphragmatic weakness). Maximal inspiratory pressure (MIP): -52 cmH2O (normal >-60). Sniff nasal inspiratory pressure (SNIP): 58 cmH2O.\n\n**ALSFRS-R Score**: 39/48 (deficits in handwriting, cutting food, dressing, walking, and climbing stairs).',
