    const Section(
      title: '+ Explain the revised El Escorial criteria and the EMG/NCS patterns that confirm ALS. What mimics must be excluded?',
      content: '**Revised El Escorial Criteria (Awaji-Shima Criteria)**:\n\nALS diagnosis requires:\n1. **Evidence of lower motor neuron (LMN) degeneration** by clinical, electrophysiologic, or neuropathologic examination\n2. **Evidence of upper motor neuron (UMN) degeneration** by clinical examination\n3. **Progressive spread** of symptoms within a region or to other regions\n4. **Absence of**: Electrophysiologic or pathologic evidence of other disease processes that explain the signs\n\n**Diagnostic Certainty Levels**:\n- **Clinically Definite ALS**: UMN + LMN signs in at least 3 body regions (bulbar, cervical, thoracic, lumbosacral)\n- **Clinically Probable ALS**: UMN + LMN signs in at least 2 regions with some UMN signs rostral to LMN signs\n- **Clinically Probable — Laboratory Supported**: UMN + LMN signs in only 1 region, OR UMN signs alone in 1 region with EMG evidence of LMN involvement in at least 2 regions\n- **Clinically Possible ALS**: UMN + LMN signs in only 1 region, or UMN signs in 2+ regions, or LMN signs rostral to UMN signs\n\n**This patient\'s classification**: He has UMN signs (hyperreflexia, Hoffman, Babinski) and LMN signs (atrophy, fasciculations, weakness) in at least 2 regions (cervical and lumbosacral), with EMG confirming LMN involvement in 3 regions. This meets **Clinically Probable or Definite ALS** depending on thoracic paraspinal EMG findings.\n\n**EMG/NCS Pattern in ALS**:\n- **Nerve conduction studies**: Reduced compound muscle action potential (CMAP) amplitudes reflecting motor axon loss. **Normal sensory nerve action potentials (SNAPs)** — this is critical. Conduction velocities are normal or mildly reduced (not below 70% of normal). No conduction block (conduction block suggests multifocal motor neuropathy, a treatable mimic).\n- **Needle EMG**: Active denervation (fibrillation potentials, positive sharp waves) and chronic reinnervation (large-amplitude, long-duration, polyphasic motor unit potentials with reduced recruitment) in at least 3 body regions. Fasciculation potentials are present. The Awaji criteria accept fasciculation potentials as equivalent to fibrillation potentials for diagnosing LMN involvement.\n\n**Critical Mimics to Exclude**:\n1. **Multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block (MMN)**: Treatable with IVIG. Key differentiator: conduction block on NCS, absent UMN signs, often asymmetric distal upper extremity weakness. Anti-GM1 antibodies present in ~50%.\n2. **Cervical spondylotic myelopathy with radiculopathy**: Can produce UMN + LMN signs. MRI cervical spine is essential. However, cervical stenosis is common incidentally — the EMG pattern must show involvement beyond a single cervical root distribution.\n3. **Kennedy disease (X-linked bulbospinal muscular atrophy)**: Slowly progressive, LMN predominant, bulbar involvement, gynecomastia, sensory neuropathy on NCS. Genetic testing for CAG repeat expansion in androgen receptor gene.\n4. **Inclusion body myositis (IBM)**: Proximal and distal weakness, especially finger flexors and quadriceps. CK mildly elevated. EMG can mimic neurogenic disease. Muscle biopsy is diagnostic.\n5. **Hyperparathyroidism, lead toxicity, thyroid disease**: Metabolic causes of motor neuron dysfunction. Check calcium, PTH, lead level, TSH.\n6. **Primary lateral sclerosis (PLS)**: Pure UMN disease. Must observe for at least 4 years without LMN signs before diagnosing PLS over ALS.\n7. **Benign fasciculation syndrome**: Fasciculations without weakness, atrophy, or denervation on EMG.\n\n**Minimum workup to exclude mimics**: MRI brain and cervical spine, complete metabolic panel, TSH, PTH, B12, serum protein electrophoresis, GM1 antibodies, CK, and consideration of genetic testing if family history or atypical features.',
    ),
    const Section(
      title: '+ The patient\'s ALSFRS-R drops 4 points over 3 months and his FVC falls to 68% predicted. Discuss respiratory management milestones.',
      content: '**Respiratory Decline in ALS — Critical Milestones and Interventions**:\n\nRespiratory failure is the leading cause of death in ALS. Proactive monitoring and timely intervention directly impact survival and quality of life.\n\n**Monitoring Schedule**: FVC, MIP, SNIP, and nocturnal oximetry should be assessed every 1-3 months depending on rate of decline.\n\n**Milestone 1 — FVC <80% predicted or symptomatic orthopnea**:\n- Initiate **nocturnal noninvasive ventilation (NIV/BiPAP)**\n- Typical starting settings: IPAP 10, EPAP 4, backup rate 12\n- Begin with nocturnal use, gradually increase to daytime as needed\n- NIV has been shown to improve survival by approximately 7-11 months and significantly improve quality of life\n- This patient at 68% FVC should already be on NIV if not started at 82%\n\n**Milestone 2 — FVC <50% predicted**:\n- NIV should be used >16 hours/day at this point for many patients\n- **Cough augmentation**: Mechanical insufflation-exsufflation (CoughAssist) device — settings typically +40/-40 cmH2O. Critical for secretion clearance and pneumonia prevention.\n- **Lung volume recruitment**: Air stacking with bag-valve-mask to maintain chest wall compliance\n- Discuss **advance directives in detail** — specifically tracheostomy decision\n- Vaccinations: influenza, pneumococcal, COVID-19\n\n**Milestone 3 — FVC <30% or NIV failure (CO2 retention, inability to tolerate NIV)**:\n- **Decision point: invasive ventilation via tracheostomy vs comfort-focused care**\n- Tracheostomy ventilation can extend survival by years but requires 24-hour caregiver support and is associated with significant caregiver burden. Only ~5-10% of ALS patients in the US choose tracheostomy.\n- If the patient declines tracheostomy, transition to **hospice and palliative care**\n- Hospice eligibility: FVC <30%, dyspnea at rest, declining to use NIV, or critically impaired nutritional status\n\n**Rapid Progression Concern in This Patient**: An ALSFRS-R decline of 4 points in 3 months is faster than the average rate (approximately 1 point per month). This suggests a more aggressive disease course. Median survival from symptom onset in ALS is 2-5 years, but rapid decliners may have shorter prognosis. The delta FVC (82% to 68% in 3 months = 14% drop) also indicates accelerated respiratory decline.\n\n**Concurrent respiratory management**:\n- **Sleep study**: Evaluate for sleep-disordered breathing and nocturnal hypoventilation (transcutaneous CO2 monitoring is preferred)\n- **Secretion management**: Glycopyrrolate or hyoscyamine for sialorrhea. Nebulized saline and suction for thick secretions.\n- **Dyspnea management**: Low-dose opioids (morphine 2.5-5 mg q4h PRN) are safe and effective for dyspnea palliation in ALS and do not significantly accelerate death when used appropriately\n- **Anxiety management**: Lorazepam 0.5-1 mg for acute air hunger episodes',
