    const Section(
      title: '+ Outline the multidisciplinary ALS clinic approach, including medication management, nutritional support with PEG timing, and adaptive equipment.',
      content: '**Multidisciplinary ALS Clinic Model**:\n\nMultidisciplinary ALS clinics have been shown to improve survival by 7-12 months and significantly improve quality of life compared to general neurology care. The team includes:\n\n**Core Team Members and Their Roles**:\n1. **Physiatrist/Neurologist**: Disease monitoring (ALSFRS-R every visit), medication management, respiratory surveillance, coordination of care\n2. **Pulmonologist**: FVC, MIP, SNIP monitoring, NIV initiation and titration, cough augmentation\n3. **Speech-Language Pathologist**: Swallowing assessment (FEES or VFSS when indicated), speech augmentation devices, communication strategies\n4. **Dietitian/Nutritionist**: Caloric needs assessment (ALS increases metabolic rate 10-20%), diet texture modification, high-calorie supplements, PEG nutrition planning\n5. **Physical Therapist**: Mobility preservation, fall prevention, stretching/ROM program, gait aids, exercise prescription (moderate intensity, avoiding overwork weakness)\n6. **Occupational Therapist**: Upper extremity adaptive equipment, ADL modifications, home safety evaluation, splinting for wrist drop\n7. **Respiratory Therapist**: NIV fitting and adherence troubleshooting, CoughAssist training, suctioning education\n8. **Social Worker**: Insurance navigation, disability application (SSDI has compassionate allowance fast-track for ALS), financial counseling, caregiver support, advance care planning facilitation\n9. **Psychologist/Psychiatrist**: Depression screening and treatment, grief counseling, caregiver mental health support\n10. **Palliative Care**: Symptom management, advance directive completion, goals of care discussions\n\n**Pharmacologic Management**:\n- **Riluzole 50 mg BID**: The only oral medication proven to extend survival in ALS (by approximately 2-3 months). Mechanism: reduces glutamate excitotoxicity. Monitor LFTs at baseline and every 3 months. Most common side effects: nausea, fatigue, elevated LFTs.\n- **Edaravone (Radicava)**: IV free radical scavenger. May slow functional decline in a subset of patients (early ALS with preserved respiratory function). Administered in 14-day cycles with 14-day breaks. Insurance approval can be challenging. Now available as an oral formulation.\n- **Tofersen**: For patients with SOD1 mutations (approximately 2% of all ALS). Intrathecal antisense oligonucleotide. Genetic testing should be offered to all ALS patients.\n- **Symptomatic medications**: Baclofen or tizanidine for spasticity, glycopyrrolate or botulinum toxin to salivary glands for sialorrhea, dextromethorphan/quinidine (Nuedexta) for pseudobulbar affect, SSRIs or SNRIs for depression\n\n**PEG Tube Timing — Critical Decision**:\n- **Discuss PEG placement when FVC is still >50%**: The procedure carries higher risk once FVC drops below 50% due to sedation risk and poor respiratory reserve. Many ALS experts recommend prophylactic PEG discussion when there is 10% or more weight loss, meal duration exceeding 30 minutes, frequent choking, or declining oral caloric intake.\n- **This patient has already lost 10 lbs (5.5% body weight)**: PEG discussion should begin now, before respiratory decline makes the procedure higher risk.\n- PEG does not replace oral feeding initially — it supplements calories. Patients can continue to eat for pleasure as long as swallowing is safe.\n- Radiologically inserted gastrostomy (RIG) or per-oral image-guided gastrostomy (PIG) are alternatives if FVC is too low for standard PEG.\n\n**Adaptive Equipment Progression**:\n- **Current needs**: Right hand — wrist splint for functional positioning, built-up grip utensils, universal cuff, voice-to-text software for work, ergonomic keyboard modifications\n- **Mobility**: Right AFO for foot drop. Eventual transition to rollator walker, then manual wheelchair, then power wheelchair with tilt/recline for pressure relief and respiratory positioning\n- **Communication**: When speech declines — speech-generating devices (SGDs). Start with high-tech AAC evaluation early while the patient can still learn the device. Eye-gaze tracking technology for late-stage communication.\n- **Home modifications**: Hospital bed with adjustable head elevation, ceiling lift for transfers, accessible bathroom, ramp for wheelchair access\n\n**Exercise Guidelines in ALS**:\n- Moderate-intensity aerobic exercise and resistance training are safe and may preserve function temporarily\n- Avoid exhaustive or eccentric exercise — overwork weakness can accelerate motor neuron loss\n- Focus on ROM, stretching, and functional maintenance rather than strengthening weakened muscles\n- Aquatic therapy is excellent for maintaining mobility with reduced joint stress',
    ),
