    const Section(
      title: '+ Discuss ALS-specific resources, disability and insurance planning, and hospice eligibility criteria.',
      content: '**Disability and Insurance Planning**:\n\n**Social Security Disability Insurance (SSDI)**:\n- ALS is on the **Social Security Compassionate Allowances list**, which fast-tracks disability approval. Unlike most conditions that require a 5-month waiting period, **ALS patients receive SSDI benefits immediately upon approval** with no waiting period (per the ALS Disability Insurance Access Act).\n- **Medicare eligibility**: ALS patients become eligible for Medicare **immediately upon SSDI approval** — the usual 24-month waiting period is waived for ALS. This is critical for covering NIV, wheelchair, PEG supplies, and home health.\n- Application should be initiated NOW while the patient can participate in the process.\n\n**Employment Considerations**:\n- **FMLA**: The patient may qualify for intermittent FMLA leave for clinic visits and symptom management while still able to work\n- **ADA reasonable accommodations**: Voice-to-text software, ergonomic modifications, flexible scheduling, telework options\n- **Short-term disability**: Through employer policy — bridge to SSDI approval\n- **Long-term disability**: Private LTD policies — review "own occupation" vs "any occupation" definitions\n\n**Financial Planning**:\n- **ALS Association**: Provides equipment loan programs, support groups, care management, respite grants, and a national helpline (1-800-782-4747)\n- **ALS-specific equipment funding**: Many ALS Association chapters provide communication devices, hospital beds, and wheelchair assistance when insurance denies coverage\n- **Veterans**: If the patient has any military service, ALS is presumptively service-connected by the VA regardless of when or where the veteran served — this provides full VA benefits\n- **Estate planning**: Encourage the patient to establish power of attorney (financial and healthcare), update his will, and complete advance directives while he has full decision-making capacity. Refer to elder law attorney.\n\n**Hospice Eligibility Criteria for ALS**:\nPatients qualify for hospice when a physician certifies a prognosis of 6 months or less if the disease follows its expected course. Specific ALS criteria include:\n1. **FVC <30% predicted** (critically impaired respiratory function)\n2. **Patient declines further mechanical ventilation** (chooses comfort over tracheostomy)\n3. **Dyspnea at rest** despite optimal NIV\n4. **Critical nutritional impairment**: Declining to receive artificial nutrition (PEG), continued weight loss despite PEG, or inability to maintain hydration\n5. **Rapid progression**: ALSFRS-R declining at a rate suggesting less than 6 months survival\n6. **Life-threatening complications**: Recurrent aspiration pneumonia, sepsis, Stage 3-4 pressure injuries\n\n**Important**: Hospice does NOT mean giving up. Palliative care should run parallel to active ALS management from the time of diagnosis. Hospice is a transition of goals from disease management to comfort maximization. Patients can revoke hospice and return to active treatment at any time.\n\n**Concurrent care model**: Some payers allow concurrent hospice and disease-modifying treatment — this is ideal for ALS patients who may want to continue riluzole while receiving hospice services.',
    ),
