    const Section(
      title: '+ Demonstrate how you would deliver the ALS diagnosis and address the prognosis question with direct, compassionate communication.',
      content: '**Breaking Bad News — SPIKES Framework Applied to ALS Diagnosis**:\n\nThis is one of the most difficult conversations in medicine. The approach must be direct without being brutal, honest without eliminating hope.\n\n**Setting and Preparation**:\n- Ensure a private, quiet room. Sit at eye level. No physical barriers between you. Phone silenced.\n- Confirm who the patient wants present — he has chosen his wife and son.\n- Assess the patient\'s understanding: he has already expressed fear of ALS, which indicates he has some preparation.\n\n**Delivering the Diagnosis**:\n\n\'Thank you for asking me directly — I respect that, and I owe you a straightforward answer.\n\nBased on the electrical studies of your nerves and muscles, your examination today, and the pattern of your symptoms over the past six months, the diagnosis is amyotrophic lateral sclerosis — ALS.\'\n\n**Pause. Allow silence. Do not fill the space with more words immediately.**\n\nWatch for the patient\'s reaction. He may cry, become silent, ask questions, or express anger. His wife and son will also be processing. Allow the emotional response to happen.\n\n**Responding to "How long do I have?"**:\n\n\'That is a question I take very seriously, and I want to answer it honestly.\n\nThe truth is that ALS affects every person differently. For most people, the disease progresses over 2 to 5 years from when symptoms first start. Some people live significantly longer — 10 years or more. A small percentage have a more rapid course.\n\nWhat I can tell you is that your breathing tests right now are in a reasonable range, your swallowing is intact, and we are catching this at a point where we can put a strong team around you. There are treatments that can help slow the disease, manage symptoms, and maintain your quality of life.\n\nI cannot give you an exact number of months or years — and honestly, anyone who does is guessing. What I can promise you is that I will be honest with you at every step. I will never withhold information from you. And I will work with you and your family to make sure you have the best possible quality of life for as long as possible.\'\n\n**Addressing the family**:\n\nTurn to the wife and son: \'I know this is devastating for all of you. This diagnosis affects the entire family. I want you to know that we will support not just [patient name], but all of you through this.\'\n\n**Establishing hope without false reassurance**:\n\n\'I want to be clear about something important — this diagnosis changes your future, but it does not end it. People with ALS continue to work, to travel, to be with their families. The research in ALS is moving faster than at any time in history. There are clinical trials available, and I will make sure you know about anything that might be relevant to you.\n\nWhat matters now is what we do next. I am going to connect you with a team of specialists who work with ALS patients every day — respiratory therapists, physical therapists, speech therapists, social workers, and others. You are not going to face this alone.\'\n\n**Before they leave**:\n- Schedule a follow-up within 1-2 weeks — the patient will have many questions after the shock has settled\n- Provide written information (ALS Association materials)\n- Ensure the patient has your contact information for questions that arise\n- Offer to call or meet with other family members who were not present\n- Begin advance directive conversation gently: \'At a future visit, when you are ready, I would like to talk about your wishes and preferences so that your care always reflects what matters most to you.\'\n\n**Key communication principles**: Warning shot before diagnosis, direct and unambiguous language, allowing silence, honoring the prognosis question with honest ranges rather than specific numbers, grounding hope in concrete next steps, acknowledging the family, and providing a clear plan forward.',
    ),
